 "notes": [
  "Hip centre = sphere fitted to the femoral head, the same code on the MRI femur, each prediction and the floor; predictions are cut at the MRI's slice planes (20 mm apart) and sampled on its 2.1 mm grid first.",
  "Only sides whose MRI femur keeps the top of its head are scored: the top slice at most 25 mm across (a cap) and the fitted sphere's top within -5..+10 mm of it. Elsewhere the head's upper part is not in the segmentation and a centre fitted to what is left sits too low.",
  "Error = prediction minus MRI, mm: anterior, medial (toward the midline) and superior components.",
  "skel_mri (the floor) is SKEL-J fitted to the MRI bones: its error is what this ground truth can resolve.",
  "slicing_shift = sliced minus unsliced centre on the same mesh: what the 20 mm slicing alone moves.",
  "Relative error uses the floor femur's length (fitted to the person's bones, not quantised by slices).",
  "Hip only: knee and ankle centres are not defined well enough on 20 mm slices.",
  "One cohort, lying down, arms overhead; SKEL-J was trained on this cohort, SKEL and OSSO were not.",
  "Selection: the subjects with a complete head against the rest on body volume, bone depth and sex (selection block). With nine subjects only a large difference would show; if the complete heads skew small, the predictor errors here are optimistic, since skin-only error grows with soft tissue.",
  "Any calibration of a predictor's offset (e.g. OSSO's anterior bias) must be fitted on separate data and only then scored here; fitting it on these hips would fit the test set. Nothing about these hips may enter the training or calibration of a predictor this bench later checks."
 ],
